  <h1>India’s Energy Transition — Balancing Coal Dependence and Renewable Push</h1>
  <div><em>Economic Times | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>India’s energy sector is at a crossroads — balancing the need for affordable, reliable power with its climate commitments. In 2024, Economic Times highlighted the tension between continued coal dependence and rapid renewable energy expansion. For UPSC, this issue links economy, environment, and governance.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Power Mix (2024): Coal ~55%, renewables ~30%, rest gas, nuclear, hydro.<br/>•	Renewable Capacity: 180+ GW installed (solar 75, wind 45, hydro 50, biomass 10).<br/>•	Target: 500 GW non-fossil capacity by 2030.<br/>•	Electricity Demand Growth: ~6% annually; peak demand >240 GW (2024).<br/>•	Coal Dependence: India 2nd largest coal consumer globally.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>1.	Global Leadership: 4th largest renewable energy capacity worldwide.<br/>2.	Solar Power Boom: World’s lowest solar tariffs (~₹2.5/unit).<br/>3.	International Solar Alliance (ISA): 110+ member countries.<br/>4.	Green Hydrogen Mission (2023): ₹19,744 crore investment push.<br/>5.	Private Sector Role: Adani, Tata, ReNew Power driving renewable growth.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Challenges<br/>1.	Coal Dependence: Needed for base-load stability, especially in summers.<br/>2.	Grid Integration: Intermittency of solar/wind straining grid.<br/>3.	Financial Health of DISCOMs: Losses ~₹80,000 crore/year.<br/>4.	Land Conflicts: Renewable projects displacing communities, wildlife.<br/>5.	Investment Gaps: Need $250 billion by 2030 for energy transition.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	National Solar Mission (2010): Flagship renewable policy.<br/>•	Renewable Energy Targets: 500 GW non-fossil by 2030.<br/>•	Perform, Achieve, Trade (PAT): Energy efficiency framework.<br/>•	Green Hydrogen Mission: Exports + domestic clean fuel.<br/>•	Energy Efficiency Financing: PACEsetter and other credit schemes.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Economic Growth: Energy crucial for industry and urbanisation.<br/>•	Climate Goals: Essential for India’s net zero by 2070.<br/>•	Social Justice: Clean energy access improves health, livelihoods.<br/>•	Strategic Autonomy: Reduces import dependence on fossil fuels.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-III (Economy & Environment): Energy, climate change.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Policy framework for energy security.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Energy transition is not a choice but a necessity.”<br/>o	“Coal keeps the lights on, but renewables hold the key to the future.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Energy politics and global climate governance.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Balanced Transition: Gradual coal phase-down with just transition for workers.<br/>2.	Grid Modernisation: Smart grids, storage, demand-side management.<br/>3.	Strengthen DISCOMs: Financial restructuring and digital reforms.<br/>4.	Investment Mobilisation: Green bonds, global climate finance.<br/>5.	Community-Centric Projects: Involve local stakeholders in renewable rollouts.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s energy transition must reconcile development needs with sustainability goals. Balancing coal’s reliability with renewable expansion will define India’s growth trajectory. For UPSC aspirants, it shows how economy, environment, and governance intertwine in policymaking.</p>
